{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 38, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Here is a 300+ word summary of the Wikipedia page \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\" without commas and with at least 3 highlighted sections in markdown format:\n\nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1147 to 1171 and a leading figure in the County of Tripoli during the mid-12th century He played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and other Crusader states\n\n**Early Life** \nHe was the son of Raymond II and Helvis of Ramlah born around 1140 his father was killed in 1147 by the Hashshashin Raymond III succeeded his father at the age of about 7\n\nRaymond III was taken under the care of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem who acted as his regent until he came of age in 1157 Baldwin III launched a successful campaign against the city of Ascalon in 1153 which was a strategic victory for the Crusaders\n\n*highlighted section part 1* **Capture and Ransom** Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din in 1170 during a battle near Aleppo he was released in 1171 after paying a large ransom this experience had a profound impact on his political outlook\n\nUpon his release Raymond III married Constance of Antioch in 1171 however the marriage was short-lived as he died later that year The exact circumstances of his death are not clear\n\nRaymond III played a crucial role in the politics of the Crusader states often finding himself at odds with King Amalric I of Jerusalem He was a strong supporter of the independence of the County of Tripoli\n\n*highlighted section part 2* **Diplomatic Relations** Raymond III maintained complex diplomatic relations with the Byzantine Empire and the Seljuk Turks seeking to secure his county against the growing threat of Nur ad-Din\n\nRaymond III was known for his military prowess and his ability to navigate the complex web of alliances in the region He worked to strengthen the defenses of the County of Tripo